/**
 * @mainpage Skip List Data Structure in C++
 *
 * @section intro Introduction
 * This project implements a Skip List, a probabilistic data structure that supports fast search, insertion,
 * and deletion operations in O(log n) average time complexity. It is an alternative to balanced binary search trees.
 *
 * @section features Features
 * - Templated skip list implementation (e.g., SkipList<int>, SkipList<string>, etc.)
 * - insert(), erase(), and find() methods
 * - Support for equality comparison (`==` and `!=`)
 * - Forward iterator support for range-based loops
 * - Google Test-based unit testing
 * - Written in modern C++ (C++20)
 *
 * @section usage Example Usage
 * @code{.cpp}
 * #include "skip_list.hpp"
 * #include <iostream>
 * 
 * int main() {
 *     SkipList<int> list;
 *     list.insert(5);
 *     list.insert(10);
 *     list.insert(7);
 * 
 *     if (list.find(10)) {
 *         std::cout << "Found 10" << std::endl;
 *     }
 * 
 *     list.erase(5);
 * 
 *     for (int value : list) {
 *         std::cout << value << " ";
 *     }
 *     return 0;
 * }
 * @endcode
 *
 * @section build How to Build and Run
 * * make        # Builds the project
 * * ./build/main
 * make test   # Builds and runs the unit tests
 *  *
 * @section context Academic Context
 * This project was developed as part of a coursework assignment in data structures and algorithms.
 * The main goal was to demonstrate understanding of probabilistic data structures and modern C++ practices.
 *
 * @section author Author
 * Created by [Mokobia Joan Chidiebere], 2025.
 */